#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134578} :activity/id #uuid "061434b6-5079-5b3b-b4da-2e73f02d7ea8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135186 :tutorial/content "The cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable is defined the same way as for a discrete random variable. \n\nGiven a continuous random variable $X,$ the CDF is the function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x).\n$$\n\nIf our random variable has the probability density function (or PDF) $f(x),$ then we have\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t) \\, \\textrm dt.\n$$\n\nNote that we use $f(t)dt$ in the integrand because $x$ serves as the upper limit of integration. \n\nFor example, consider the random variable $X$ with the following PDF:\n\n![](images/e7/e79c230f2b548c9280425f555f6d99a8f997e989289a05fb5f4584b656c4dcc8.svg)\n\nSince $f(x)= 0$ for all $x < 0,$  we have\n\n$$\nF(x)  = \\int_{-\\infty}^x 0 \\, \\textrm dt = 0\n$$\n\nif $x<0.$\n\nTo compute the CDF for $x\\geq 0,$  we integrate $f(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{2}{\\pi (1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\frac{2}{\\pi }(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing our CDF as a function defined over the entire real line, we get\n\n![](images/66/66b7bf54271075ae72fcf07fa4dc8573a94e97c2b2cc77680c0ec94b6053f360.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "de1bbdf6-687d-537a-a6fe-03d9fafbe841" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3f773be7-dfa4-524f-aec7-8ade124c5cea" :step/math-academy-id 132590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133726 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133516 :question/id #uuid "38b4473a-f956-5af5-863b-387dc10b4924" :question/math-academy-id "e-13362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d7509fa-a254-5b78-b307-3e0c4ef4d9ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424145}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With a Lower Bound"} :step/id #uuid "2411aa47-0b30-5e9c-b541-4d44b5f7aeed" :step/math-academy-id 132591 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133727 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133517 :question/id #uuid "98dd834c-ac74-5cdd-88ee-060fd9ac689d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b0ce833-e045-5aff-94a4-f9ad56cdb904" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424886}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a CDF Given a PDF That’s Nonzero Over All Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f242a3a5-626f-54b6-b0cc-b8af4506a3f0" :step/math-academy-id 132594 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135187 :tutorial/content "When a random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive on the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ only, we have\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 0$ for $x < a,$ and\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 1$ for $x > b.$\n\nIn these cases, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/7a/7a64bac7c252a3f52fc92f6364edb5c66f31e47da28e78eeb0d069d894135700.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0794419f-6b72-568b-a787-d71121c8bcd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8365 :tutorial/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds"} :step/id #uuid "a74ce991-870f-5bfa-8a96-8bbed06ce5b6" :step/math-academy-id 132597 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133728 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133518 :question/id #uuid "4159a407-ea9f-5728-b219-bf1afab732a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-13364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eedcfa3d-b02b-5f96-99f4-cd0a452d272a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424888}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds"} :step/id #uuid "47bc1334-f06b-5cc0-b191-427d1d862db4" :step/math-academy-id 132598 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135188 :tutorial/content "As with discrete random variables, we can use the CDF of a continuous random variable to quickly compute a probability over an interval:\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = F(b) - F(a)\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a random variable $X$ has the following CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}, \\qquad -\\infty < x < \\infty.\n$$\n\nWe can use the above CDF to quickly compute $P(0 \\leq x \\leq 1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le x\\le 1) & =F(1)-F(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.231\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval, so we have\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq X \\leq 1) = P(0 < X \\leq 1) = P(0 \\leq X < 1) = P(0 < X < 1) = 0.231.\n$$\n\nIn general, for a continuous random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = P(a < X < b) = F(b) - F(a).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5203710-822b-55d4-9dfb-985bc4d41d6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8366 :tutorial/title "Using a CDF to Compute a Probability Over an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "55418ca8-74f9-5755-9f75-2d411c4a476e" :step/math-academy-id 132601 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133729 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133519 :question/id #uuid "707e3b49-9c13-50d8-8a7f-c4d5ec44e0ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-13365"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbc399b-90f3-5acd-ac70-7345a8436978" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424887}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using a CDF"} :step/id #uuid "681dc276-df96-5b4d-b26c-338d9da62b88" :step/math-academy-id 132602}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134025}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424891} :activity/id #uuid "dc977e81-fd96-5729-9752-b43ae05cc6d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "492f777b-8264-55ab-9be1-d1359bd9513d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424871} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7a5540-6762-5260-9b7f-2a98061bf95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424872} {:answer/id #uuid "50e4b7f0-64ef-587b-bfde-a4520b836560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424873} {:answer/id #uuid "d60102cb-368c-5209-b3c3-06d9c44b89a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424880} {:answer/id #uuid "ef55c585-a616-50c9-9382-ffb7b5a8ceda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424881} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce55fccd-c2b9-544b-881d-b59a7e7c0f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b70eaac-0c45-5da5-8a2f-2002d9071035" :question/math-academy-id "q-89727" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/3|}}{6},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3f2429d9-8a43-540c-8ee1-21b8c07473a4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424890}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f33f1e-6e04-5ef0-8a18-a9a9635e495a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424869} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc40166-55c4-53b3-bf06-562cddc8e868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424875} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0df617-e565-5181-bd27-09321bb7abfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424877} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e9b386-cf97-5734-82c8-f8b9318ea865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424878} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbc1dbf-3cbe-5835-99b1-f9050b0ed224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}-\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424875} :answer-field/id #uuid "5340e926-2580-5f2f-bf08-46d25e342346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "97c91681-ff70-5452-bbfe-e1a92bb2e9d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126156" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}(x-1)(x-4),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}(t-1)(t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}({t}^{2}-5t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2}{27}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{t}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}t)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x-(-\\frac{2}{27}+\\frac{5}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9bf4404a-eca3-5f58-9aa8-59a46fdb29aa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d43dadc-2415-595b-83bd-07293e62a4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424868} {:answer/id #uuid "2d728c61-ee7a-5242-b976-dff68318b24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424870} {:answer/id #uuid "54302da3-4ffd-5ae6-9474-e5f95bb705f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424874} {:answer/id #uuid "86635ef4-b567-5842-a8be-7564b4bc47cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424876} {:answer/id #uuid "c53339f9-decf-5456-b59e-339275bad539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424870} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2e7998-6dc3-5838-ae14-0d427c2b4449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d72a8e6-eda0-59be-829b-e0298d5412e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-89695" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X\\le 5).$" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nHence, the required probability is $F(5)=1-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{4}{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b81f8974-1a7b-53cb-bb5f-1b47021e293e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424889}}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424867}]}
